WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.731 --> 00:00:03.153
- [Narrator] The MISSISSIPPI
RIVER is drying up.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.600
Water levels are approaching record lows,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.054
BARGES are getting stuck on sandbars

00:00:08.054 --> 00:00:10.140
in unprecedented numbers.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.080
Many ports and docks no
longer have water deep enough

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.540
for commercial boats to safely reach them.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.033
And this is a problem
for the whole country.

00:00:18.934 --> 00:00:21.000
- The MISSISSIPPI RIVER a lot of people

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.770
just think of it as this beautiful

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.440
large historic river in
the middle of the country,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.040
but really it serves as
a transportation highway

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.798
for a lot of industry.

00:00:30.798 --> 00:00:32.880
- [Narrator] The river is
a critical shipping route

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.430
for agriculture, oil
and building materials.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.870
And the MISSISSIPPI WATERSHED

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.390
which includes all the
creeks, streams, and lakes

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.130
that feed into the MISSISSIPPI RIVER

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.750
is the largest in the US.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.040
- Farmers in Iowa,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.570
they harvest their soybeans

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.610
and they put them on
BARGES and send them down

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.350
to the port below New Orleans,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.820
and then that's sent to China.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.910
Chemicals are processed in Louisiana

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.650
and sent it up river to the Ohio

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.270
to factories in Pittsburgh.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.740
The central part of this country

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.920
thrives in part on industry,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.070
moving goods and services
up and down the river.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.360
- [Narrator] October is traditionally

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.310
a month of low water on the Mississippi.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.260
But this year, the rivers water levels

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.200
are approaching lows not seen since 1988.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.610
- And if that's the case,

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.900
the river at certain points

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.570
is just gonna shut down
for commercial traffic.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.130
- [Narrator] The water has gotten so low

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.480
that a shipwreck was exposed

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.650
along the banks of the river

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.200
in Baton Rouge, Louisiana
earlier this month.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.711
Archaeologists believe it's sank in 1915.

00:01:28.711 --> 00:01:31.199
In recent weeks, there
have been over 20 blockages

00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:34.620
on the river and backups
of 1000s of BARGES per day.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.320
- When a barge gets stuck on a sandbar,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.750
it's a real expensive mess.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.520
Another barge has to come,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.380
you have to bring equipment

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.930
to remove that material

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.266
and put it in another barge

00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:49.920
to the point where it will float enough

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.230
so that it can get off the sandbar.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.000
- [Narrator] In some cases the TOWBOATS,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.950
which pull dozens of BARGES each

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.600
have hit sandbar so hard

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.090
that the BARGES they're towing disconnect.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.180
And that's dangerous for
bridges and other boats.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.100
Riverboat cruises have also halted

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.540
due to low water levels.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.650
Industry leaders say,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.245
if water levels stay this
low or get even lower,

00:02:10.245 --> 00:02:12.933
the tourism industry
will take a massive hit.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.170
So what's causing the river to dry up?

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.330
- [Cameron] It just did not
get enough rain this year,

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.460
particularly in the
upper OHIO RIVER VALLEY.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.474
Normally, this is hurricane
season in the south.

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:25.620
Some hurricanes will plow
into the United States

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.447
and then become enormous rainstorms

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:30.360
that go up into the OHIO RIVER VALLEY

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.590
and dump a lot of rain.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.270
That didn't happen this year.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.920
- [Narrator] And industries
that rely on the river

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.680
to transport goods don't
have many alternatives.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.760
- It's not as though a farmer

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.680
can just run to the railroad

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:41.880
and that solves the problem.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.430
The rail system is so pressed right now

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.410
and they've have labor shortage issues.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.260
They have capacity issues
that they're running into.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.370
- [Narrator] But sticking with the river

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.990
will be costly for farmers.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.240
Normally, shipping along
the MISSISSIPPI RIVER

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.470
is the cheaper option.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.430
But with fewer boats

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.880
and BARGES able to transport
goods prices have shot up.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.580
For example, the cost of
sending a ton of corn,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.930
soybeans, or other grains

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.910
from St. Louis to southern Louisiana

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:09.676
has jumped over 380% since October 2021.

00:03:09.676 --> 00:03:11.520
The fall harvest season,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.080
which usually peaks in October

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.506
is the busiest time for
agricultural products.

00:03:15.506 --> 00:03:17.138
- If you can't get a rail line out,

00:03:17.138 --> 00:03:19.770
your soybeans are just
sitting in an open field.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.838
I mean, a lot of farmers

00:03:20.838 --> 00:03:24.963
are hesitating to even harvest right now.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.260
Let's say there are massive rainstorms

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.450
and your soybeans are
just sitting out there,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.790
that could really, really
really be disastrous.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.290
- [Narrator] This
disruption along the river

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.120
comes at a time when the war in Ukraine

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.470
has prompted huge demand

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.791
for American agricultural products.

00:03:38.791 --> 00:03:42.180
- And farmers are very excited.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.280
They can't get their stuff to market.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.240
They can't get it to market.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.253
Not easily and not cheaply.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.560
- [Narrator] Over the last few weeks,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.780
the U.S. COAST GUARD
has imposed restrictions

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.880
on the depth that
commercial boats and BARGES

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:54.960
can sit in the water,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.660
and on the number of
BARGES that tows can pull.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.730
Both actions are to try to stop BARGES

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.170
and boats from getting stuck.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.840
But industry leaders say it's not enough.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.700
- There's no real answer
except for the rain.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.520
There's hope that
beginning around December,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.570
rain will start to fall
in the OHIO RIVER VALLEY

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.147
to an extent where it'll start to bring

00:04:15.147 --> 00:04:17.160
that water back down to the Mississippi

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.720
and things will get better.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.900
If not, we're all in a lot of trouble.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.983
(gentle light music)

